FreeJ2ME-Plus's core unpacks its Java runtime, a .tar.gz on Linux, with the
system's tar and "-xf", which leaves GNU tar and bsdtar to see the gzip on
their own. BusyBox tar does not: on plastron (the GKD Pixel 2's system) it
answered "invalid tar magic" and the game never started. "-xzf" says it, and
every tar the core can meet off Windows takes it; the archive is always a
.tar.gz there (RUNTIME_ARCHIVE_EXT).

For ZyperOS, mk/build-freej2me.sh applies it; GPL-3.0 as the code it
changes.

--- a/src/libretro/bundled_jre.c
+++ b/src/libretro/bundled_jre.c
@@ -195,7 +195,7 @@
 
 	if (pid == 0)
 	{
-		execlp("tar", "tar", "-xf", archive, "-C", dest, (char*)NULL);
+		execlp("tar", "tar", "-xzf", archive, "-C", dest, (char*)NULL);
 		_exit(127);
 	}
 	if (pid < 0 || waitpid(pid, &status, 0) < 0)
